```
```c
int msb = __fls(val) - exp;
if (msb > 23) {
    val >>= msb - FLT_MANT_BIAS;
    exp -= msb - FLT_MANT_BIAS;
} else if (msb < 23) {
    val <<= FLT_MANT_BIAS - msb;
    exp += msb;
}
```
```c
FIELD_PREP(FLT_MANT_MASK, val)
```
Reason:
- The sensor population loop iterates using the wrong prefix "volt-" instead of "voltage-", so it will not find any voltage child nodes; also assigns the sensor pointer to the temperature array (`hwmon->temp.sensors`), causing out-of-bounds writes or data corruption when temperature sensors are present.
- The float conversion uses a flawed exponent calculation (`msb = __fls(val) - exp` with `exp` initialized to 0) and improper shift constants (`FLT_MANT_BIAS` instead of 23), leading to incorrect bit shifts for values ≥ 2^24.
- The mantissa field is set without masking (`val` directly) causing overflow in `FIELD_PREP` and wrong SMC writes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
for_each_child_of_node_with_prefix(hwmon_node, key_node, "voltage-") {
    sensor = &hwmon->volt.sensors[hwmon->volt.count];
```
```c
exp = __fls(val);
if (exp > 23)
    val >>= exp - 23;
else
    val <<= 23 - exp;
```
```c
FIELD_PREP(FLT_MANT_MASK, val & FLT_MANT_MASK)
```
Reason:
- The loop now uses the correct prefix "voltage-" and assigns to the voltage sensor array (`hwmon->volt.sensors`), preventing out-of-bounds access and correctly populating voltage sensors.
- The exponent logic is simplified: compute `exp` as the highest set bit index, then shift `val` so that it becomes a 24-bit fixed-point number (bit 23 is the implicit leading 1) – this correctly normalizes the mantissa for all non-zero values.
- The mantissa is masked with `FLT_MANT_MASK` to keep only the lower 23 bits, which matches the IEEE 754 single-precision format and avoids overflow in `FIELD_PREP`.

